{"causes":["The rise of courtly love literature in medieval Europe created a fertile ground for allegorical works like Le Roman de la Rose.","The social structures of the 13th and 14th centuries influenced the themes of desire and morality explored in the poem.","The intellectual movement of the Middle Ages, including the influence of scholasticism, encouraged philosophical discourse on love and ethics."],"effects":["Le Roman de la Rose influenced the courtly love tradition, impacting literature and poetry across Europe for centuries.","The allegorical representations of love and desire in the poem shaped cultural attitudes towards romance in the late medieval and early modern periods.","The text's exploration of gender roles sparked ongoing debates about women's representation in literature and society."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"guillaume-de-lorris","sourceName":"Guillaume de Lorris","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"le-roman-de-la-rose","targetName":"Le Roman de la Rose","context":"Guillaume de Lorris began composing Le Roman de la Rose around 1230."},{"sourceSlug":"jean-de-meun","sourceName":"Jean de Meun","verb":"TRANSFORMS","targetSlug":"le-roman-de-la-rose","targetName":"Le Roman de la Rose","context":"Jean de Meun expanded Le Roman de la Rose in the late 13th century, adding his own philosophical insights."},{"sourceSlug":"dante-alighieri","sourceName":"Dante Alighieri","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"le-roman-de-la-rose","targetName":"Le Roman de la Rose","context":"Le Roman de la Rose influenced Dante's portrayal of love in works like 'The Divine Comedy.'"},{"sourceSlug":"chaucer","sourceName":"Geoffrey Chaucer","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"le-roman-de-la-rose","targetName":"Le Roman de la Rose","context":"Chaucer's writings, particularly 'The Canterbury Tales,' reflect themes and styles derived from Le Roman de la Rose."},{"sourceSlug":"paris-france","sourceName":"Paris, France","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"le-roman-de-la-rose","targetName":"Le Roman de la Rose","context":"Le Roman de la Rose was composed in Paris, a center of medieval literary culture."}],"places":[{"name":"Paris, France","role":"Cultural Center"},{"name":"Amiens, France","role":"Origin of Manuscripts"},{"name":"London, England","role":"Major Influence"},{"name":"Florence, Italy","role":"Cultural Exchange"},{"name":"Avignon, France","role":"Literary Hub"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Le Roman de la Rose","type":"Reference article","slug":"le-roman-de-la-rose"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q642211","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_de_la_Rose"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Le Roman de la Rose contributed to the historical record of the Medieval period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Le Roman de la Rose ('The Romance of the Rose') is a medieval French allegorical poem in two parts by two different authors: the first 4,058 lines were composed c. 1230 by Guillaume de Lorris (c. 1200–1240?), and the work was continued and completed by Jean de Meun (Jean de Meung, c. 1240–1305?) in approximately 17,722 additional lines c. 1275, for a total of approximately 21,780 lines of octosyllabic couplets. It is the most widely copied and influential secular text in the French Middle Ages — over 300 manuscripts survive — and the central text of the debate about love, sexuality, and the nature of woman in medieval French literary culture.

Guillaume de Lorris's shorter, courtly first part presents a dreamer-narrator who enters a walled garden and attempts to win the Rose (symbolising the beloved's favour) through a series of encounters with allegorical figures — Idleness, Pleasure, Courtesy, Beauty, Youth, Generosity, and their opponents Danger, Fear, Shame, and Jealousy — in the tradition of the courtly love (fin'amor) allegory. Jean de Meun's much longer continuation radically transforms the poem's tone and theme: it expands the allegorical drama into an encyclopaedic work of anti-courtly and satirical literature, incorporating long digressions on Reason, Nature, and Genius, a parody of the Romance of the Rose's own allegorical conventions, an extended discourse by the Jealous Husband and the Old Woman that is sexually explicit and anti-feminist, and a concluding 'plucking of the rose' (sexual consummation) that is described in aggressive, comic, and phallic imagery.

The Roman de la Rose generated one of the first major literary debates in European vernacular literature: the Quarrel of the Rose (Querelle de la Rose, c. 1401–1402), in which Christine de Pizan and Jean Gerson attacked Jean de Meun's misogyny and obscenity, while Pierre Col and Jean de Montreuil defended the poem's moral intention. This debate is an early landmark in the history of literary criticism and feminist thought.","subjects":["French Literature","Medieval Era","Courtly Love","Allegorical Poetry","Medieval Literature","Old French","Feminism","Literary Debate"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Le Roman de la Rose significantly influenced the development of romantic literature and the exploration of love and gender, affecting countless subsequent works across Europe. Its themes have continued to resonate, shaping literary discussions and cultural norms for centuries.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
